-- Kat Infection support. Built for an infection round game: one side is turned
-- and hunts the rest, so the thing that actually matters is knowing where the
-- infected are and how close the nearest one is.
--
-- tag is what marks an infected player. Left blank it matches a team or a
-- character part whose name contains "infect" or "kat", which covers the usual
-- naming -- but games rename things, so it is a text box rather than a
-- hardcoded guess. Anything typed there wins.
-- Lucky World. Block-breaking game, so everything here is about finding the
-- block you want and hitting it: which ones are nearby, getting to one, and
-- keeping a tool swinging at it.
--
-- The filter is a text box rather than a hardcoded list because block names are
-- the game's business, not this menu's -- and a name that changes with an
-- update should cost you a retype, not a rewrite.
-- Remote spy and replayer. Every remote the game fires is recorded with its
-- arguments; any recorded call can then be fired again, as many times as you
-- like, in the same frame.
--
-- That last part is the whole dupe: two break requests arriving before either
-- finishes, which is the same race two players produce by clicking together.
-- Firing the game's own call with the game's own arguments means nothing has
-- to be reverse engineered -- the client already knew how to ask.
-- Chest auto-hold. Right-click a chest and it holds right-click on that chest
-- until the chest is gone, then stops.
--
-- The claimed chest is remembered by INSTANCE, not by name or by whatever is
-- under the cursor: a chunk full of chests all called "Chest" means the moment
-- another one drifts under the pointer, a name-based version starts breaking
-- that one instead. It is also marked on screen, so which chest is claimed is
-- never a guess.
-- Exploit detector, client side. It watches what other players' characters
-- actually do, which is the one thing an exploiter cannot hide: their movement
-- is replicated to everyone, and a client that flies, teleports or sprints at
-- 200 studs a second looks exactly like that from here.
--
-- Being honest about the limits, because they matter for what you do with it:
-- this sees MOVEMENT, not intent. Server lag, a game's own launch pads, and
-- vehicles all produce the same readings, which is why it takes repeated
-- strikes rather than flagging on one bad frame. It is evidence to look at,
-- not a verdict -- the real one belongs on the server, where nothing can lie
-- about it in the first place.
